{{escapeMarkdown project.description}}

This is a local MCP server with one example tool. It runs only on this computer
and does not require internet access while running.
It is a learning and connectivity template, not a finished business server.

## What this server can do

The `hello` tool returns a short greeting. With the optional name `Mirďas`, the
structured response is:

```json
{
  "message": "Hello, Mirďas!"
}
```

Without a name it returns:

```json
{
  "message": "Hello from your local MCP server!"
}
```

## What this server cannot do

- It does not read, write, or delete files.
- It does not use the internet or make network requests.
- It does not run shell commands.
- It does not read environment secrets.
- It does not send data away from this computer.

## Safety overview

| Capability | Default |
| --- | --- |
| Read files | Denied |
| Write or delete files | Denied |
| Network access | Denied |
| Shell commands | Denied |
| Environment secrets | Not used |

These boundaries come from the generated code, its limited tool input schema,
and the permissions declared to Forge. They are not an operating-system
sandbox.

## Install

```bash
pnpm install --frozen-lockfile
pnpm build
```

The first installation may need registry access. Installation without internet
works only when every dependency is already present in the local pnpm store.
After installation and build, the server itself works offline.

## Run

```bash
pnpm start
```

This starts the local `{{server.transport}}` server from `dist/index.js`. The
process speaks MCP over standard input and output, so a terminal normally shows
no user interface.

## Connect an MCP client

Use the local Node.js executable and the absolute path to this project's built
entrypoint:

```json
{
  "command": "/path/to/node",
  "args": [
    "/path/to/my-local-server/dist/index.js"
  ]
}
```

Replace both example paths with real local paths. `npx` is not needed and this
server is not published to npm. LM Studio and other MCP clients may wrap these
same `command` and `args` values in their own configuration format.

## Verify

Run the local unit test:

```bash
pnpm test
```

After connecting an MCP client, list the available tools and call `hello` with
no input or with a short optional `name`. The server should return one of the
responses shown above.

## Make changes

The example tool is stored in `src/tools/hello.ts`. The MCP registration and
stdio startup are in `src/index.ts`. Future Forge versions will be able to add
and configure more tools for you; this template intentionally contains only
one small example.
